  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 158 AM EST Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Surface high pressure will build into the area today in \nthe wake of cold front.  The center of the high will gradually shift \neastward through Wednesday, bringing dry conditions and noticeably \ncolder temperatures.  This morning, temperatures will start off in \nthe 20s for much of the area, with north/northwest winds 5-10 knots. \nThis will be quite the contrast with yesterday morning, when \ntemperatures were around 15 degrees warmer.  The winds will drop \nwind chills into the teens for the northern Adirondacks and \nnortheastern Vermont.  Mountain tops will be much colder however, \nwith wind chills near or below zero.  Skies will start off partly \ncloudy, but will clear through the day as high pressure builds in. \nDespite increasing sun, temperatures will only reach into the 30s \ntoday.  \n\nTonight, the center of the surface high will shift directly overhead \nand ideal radiational cooling conditions will set up.  The clear \nskies and calm winds will allow low temperatures Wednesday morning \nto drop into the teens for most areas, except locally in the 20s \nalong and in the immediate vicinity of Lake Champlain.  Highs \nWednesday will be a few degrees warmer than Tuesday, but still \nmainly in the 30s to low 40s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A low pressure system will come up the Atlantic Seaboard on \nThanksgiving day. The primary impacts from this system will be from the \nhigh winds and wet roads during the holiday travel period. While we \ndon't expect widespread frozen precipitation, some freezing rain is \npossible at the very front end of the storm, specifically in the Saint \nLawrence Valley. We could see further limited wintry mix Thursday night \nand Friday morning above 1000ft. As the system progresses, the \ntransportation concerns will be limited as thermal profiles of the \nstorm predominantly favor rain. While temperatures will be above \nfreezing, they will not be warm, leading to recreational impacts. \nFriday is shaping up to be unpleasant with cold rain and gusty winds. A \n850mb jet will bring 60kt+ winds to the summits of the Greens and \nAdirondacks, with gusty winds reaching down into the valley floors. The \nensemble qpf spread is indicating a 25-75 percentile precip range from \n0.5 to 1.5 inches, with the heaviest rainfall in southern Vermont. The \nrainfall should prove to be beneficial and eat away at some of our D0 \nand D1 drought.",
